STC89C52-Memory Layer & Control

 1. Memory Layer
I worked on the memory part of the microcontroller simulator using Java.
I used an Array to store the memory values and program instructions. The Memory class contains two types of memory:

Data Memory: Stores data values.
Program Memory: Stores the instructions of the program.

The data memory has 256 locations and the program memory also has 256 locations.
The memory can be reset using the `reset()` method. This clears the stored data and instructions.
The readData() and writeData() methods are used to read and store data in memory.
Te readInstruction() and writeInstruction() methods are used to read and store program instructions.

2. Instruction Representation

The Instruction class is used to store information about an instruction.
It contains:
* Instruction name
*  Operand
* Category

The getFullInstruction() method displays the complete instruction with its operand.
For example:
MOV A, 10
The `toString()` method is also used to display the instruction easily.

 3. Instructions Implemented
As my assigned task, I worked on four instructions:

MOV
MOV is used to transfer data from one place to another.
Category: Data Transfer
Operand: Register/Data
Operation: Moves the given value to the required location.
Flags: No flags are affected.

STORE
STORE is used to save a value into memory.
Category: Data Transfer
Operand: Memory address
Operation: Stores the value in the specified memory location.
Flags: No flags are affected.

JMP
JMP is used to change the program flow.
Category: Control Flow
Operand: Address
Operation: Changes the Program Counter to the given address.
Flags: No flags are affected.

HALT
HALT is used to stop the program.
Category: Program Termination
Operand: None
Operation: Stops the execution of the program.
Flags: No flags are affected.

4. Java Classes Used
Instruction.java
The Instruction class stores the name, operand and category of an instruction.
 Memory.java
The Memory class manages the data memory and program memory. It also provides methods to read, write and reset the memory.

5. Result
The memory part of the simulator was implemented using Java arrays.
Program instructions can be stored and accessed through program memory. 
The instruction class also makes it easier to represent and display instructions.

This completes my Week 2 work for the Memory Layer & Control part of the project.
